   **This layout is literal.** Keep it as a block.
7. **Block until the literal reply `approved`.** No timeout, no implicit acceptance; a question, silence, or a partial reply is never transcribed as approval.
8. **Create the task list immediately on approval** — one task per planned step, `0:` marked completed. Nothing runs in between.

**The `design-interview:` flag.** `design-interview: true | false` is standing Step-0 configuration, default `true`, printed in the confirmation display above and recorded in plan frontmatter beside `walk:`. It is not derived from anything: `false` is the **standing Step-0 form** of Step 2's user-only interview waiver, carrying that waiver's whole force and none of it weakened — the run proceeds without the Design Interview because the user said so. A standing form is **not a second way to discharge that waiver**: the reason is still written verbatim into the design's assumptions, quoting the Step-0 reply, and the flag itself records with attribution — `design-interview: false <user> <date>`, the sibling literal of `design-waived:` and `rigor-override:`, because that attribution is the only trace that a human made the call. `design-interview:` is recorded, not validated — no hook parses it, unlike `walk:`, whose enum blocks at write time, and `rigor-override:`, whose presence the floor checks read; **an agent never sets it**, in either direction, and a later reader meets a decision rather than an absent interview.

**The review fields.** Step 0 writes `review-cadence:`, `fix-policy:` and `fix-cap:` into the plan header beside `rigor:`, each with the value its rigor sets (the table in `SKILL.md`): `single` writes `once`, `S1,S2-on` and `2`; `double` writes `once`, `S1,S2-on` and `10%` of the plan's rows. Each is the user's to set with the Override DSL: `set fix-policy=<ratings>` takes `S1 S1-on S1-off S2 S2-on S2-off S3 S4`, comma-joined, `set fix-cap=<n>` takes a number, and `set review-cadence=once` names the one cadence there is. A value the user sets is read verbatim and never widened by an agent.

**The `permissions` line.** In bypass and auto mode an engaged run answers the platform's permission questions from each asker's workspace; in any other mode the platform's dialog reaches the user as before. Approving this card is the consent; `permission-answers: false` in `.bionic/config.yaml` turns it off.

**Task-list format — blessed, and it binds across every step, not just Step 0.**

- **Chronological order of execution.** The list reads top to bottom as the work will actually happen.
- **Format A — `<task>: <short desc>`** (e.g. `3: Plan — lock tasks and matrix`).
- **Format B — `<task>/<task>: <short desc>`**, only where tasks are involved (e.g. `4/2: fix the window conversion`).
- **A takes precedence over B when tasks are present**: the step's own entry is DROPPED and its tasks stand in its place. Never print `4: Implement` above `4/1: …` — display space is precious and the parent line is duplicative. It is also a line that can never complete until all its tasks do, so it carries no signal.
- **Short descriptions.** The subject is a signal, not a summary — detail belongs in the task body or the plan.
- **No filler.** Every entry is a real unit of work with a real status.
- **No freeform titles.** An entry without a `<task>:` or `<task>/<task>:` prefix is malformed.
- Mark `in_progress` on starting and `completed` the moment it is done — never batch completions. When a subagent finishes, the orchestrator updates the entry; dispatched work is ledgered in the plan, never as a bare task.

The list is the user's visible progress surface. No hook can read a task list, so the format is the whole discipline. The per-task expansion happens at plan approval; see Step 3.

**Override DSL.** `set <axis|flag>=<value>` and `set verify(<AC>)=<tier>`, comma-separated, ending in `confirm`. `explain [axis]` renders the axis tables and never counts as confirmation; a free-text question about the display is treated as `explain`, never as a parse error. A rigor override BELOW a derivable floor is **accepted, never refused** — no code refuses it, and neither does this skill. Before locking, name the binding floor, the evidence class it was buying ("double gives `evidence` its own reader; without it, one critic holds every question"), and what specifically is given up; then proceed with the user's value and record `rigor-override: <user> <date> derived=<v> chosen=<v>` in frontmatter, so every later reader sees a decision rather than a derivation error. The regression setting is the sibling: `session-poker.sh regression <yes|no> '<reply>'` writes `regression:` and, when the value differs from the scale default, records `regression-override: <user> <date> derived=<v> chosen=<v>` in frontmatter. The floor checks read the rigor marker and log `user-overridden` instead of a violation. Advisory strength scales with the floor's reason — the security/privacy flag floor gets the strongest language here — but there are **no carve-outs**: every floor accepts the override.

**Evidence:** `Step 0: configured at <ISO> via <reply>; model_plan=<tiers>`

